---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import JourneyMap from "../../components/JourneyMap.astro";
import Distribution from "../../components/Distribution.astro";
import { u } from "../../lib/url.js";
import w from "../../data/walk.json";
const S = { written:"Written", next:"Being written next", queued:"Queued" };
---
<Base
  title="Walk the line"
  eyebrow="One line, five regions"
  dek="One itinerary follows the descent in the order it happened, across four countries. The other five go deep on a single region. Not a map of dots — an order to do things in, with the drive between them, what is publicly visible, what needs a key, and what this archive still has no photograph of.">

  <section class="prose">
    <p>Every other page here was written at a distance: a register read through a screen, a surname in an index, a book quoted in a footnote. These pages are for going. They assume a car, a week, and a willingness to knock on a door in a language you half speak.</p>
    <p><strong>Start with «The Line, end to end».</strong> The five regional itineraries were built one valley and one coast at a time, which is the right shape for research and the wrong shape for a descent — it put Gračišće in one walk and Senj in another, when they are the same family. The line walk puts them back in order and the regional ones become the deep-dives off it.</p>
    <p>Each one ends with the same two lists — <strong>what to ask for</strong>, and <strong>what to photograph that we do not have</strong>. The second list is the honest one. It is where this archive is thin.</p>
  </section>

  <ul class="wl">{w.lines.map(l => (
    <li class={"wl-i s-" + l.status}>
      <div class="lab">{S[l.status]}</div>
      <h3>{l.href ? <a href={u(l.href)}>{l.name}</a> : l.name}</h3>
      <p class="dek">{l.dek}</p>
      {l.href && <p class="mono"><a href={u(l.href)}>Open the itinerary →</a></p>}
    </li>
  ))}</ul>

  {/* 12 November 2026 (the page merges, PR B): /journeys/ is this section now; /journeys/ redirects to /walk/#journeys. The text below is
      the whole of the old page, in its own order; its title, eyebrow and dek are the section's heading, label and lead. */}
  <Rule name="arrows" label="Journeys" />
  <section class="blk" id="journeys">
    <h2>Journeys</h2>
    <p class="lab">Carnia → Istria → the world</p>
    <p class="dek">Three movements, five centuries: weavers walking south out of the mountains, two men arriving at one port in the same eighteen months, and a twentieth-century scatter across four continents.</p>
  </section>

<section class="blk">
  <JourneyMap />
  <p class="cap">Schematic; positions approximate to real geography, coastline omitted. The next build replaces this with a real map carrying the Franciscan Cadastre of 1817–20 and the Austrian military surveys as overlays, so a journey can be traced across the map the traveller would have recognised.</p>
</section>
<Rule name="arrows" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The road out of Carnia</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Carnia's economy ran on emigration for three centuries. The <em>cramars</em> — itinerant pedlars — went north and east, as far as Hungary and Transylvania; some 68.7 per cent of emigrants from the Gorto valleys went to Austria and southern Germany. The weavers and tailors went the other way, down into the Veneto and into Istria.</p>
    <p>From the parishes of Ovaro and Luincis a long series of weavers took that road. The documents become numerous in the seventeenth century and frequent in the eighteenth, and alongside the artisans went clergy, schoolmasters, artists and musicians. Migration was seasonal at first — out in autumn, home in late spring — and in some cases became permanent.</p>
    <p>This matters because it turns two unconnected origins into one story. Mione and Gologorica are not separate dots on a map; they are the two ends of a documented labour migration that was running at exactly the time a Valentino Defranceschi came out of Carnia and was made a citizen of Fiume.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<Rule name="pomegranate" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>Where they ended up</h2>
  <p class="dek">The fused spelling, by country. Italy and the United States lead, but the Croatian figure is the one that matters — it has been falling for seventy years.</p>
  <div style="margin-top:22px"><Distribution spelling="Defranceschi" /></div>
</section>
  <Sources ids={["familysearch", "album2012", "arolsen"]} />
</Base>

<style>
  .wl{list-style:none;margin:34px 0 0;padding:0;display:grid;gap:12px}
  .wl-i{background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-left:3px solid var(--rule-strong);
    border-radius:3px;padding:18px 22px}
  .wl-i.s-written{border-left-color:var(--accent)}
  .wl-i.s-next{border-left-color:var(--ochre)}
  .wl-i h3{font-size:23px;margin:6px 0 6px}
  .wl-i .dek{max-width:70ch}
  .wl-i .mono{margin-top:10px}
</style>
